    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d226">

                <pb facs="0753" n="1543" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v01p2/pg1543"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.00/9–2553</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Counselor (MacArthur) to the Secretary of
                            State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1953-09-25">September 25, 1953</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Mr. Secretary: I understand it was not feasible to discuss with the President
                    yesterday the question of American prestige abroad. The memorandum to the
                    President which I prepared for your consideration was returned to me. I am
                    attaching it hereto because I don’t know precisely how to proceed without
                    further guidance from you. The memorandum envisaged transmitting copies of
                    Ambassador Dillon’s and Ambassador Aldrich’s report.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d226-f1"/> This seemed the best way to handle the matter since the
                    hard core of free world strength outside of the U.S. rests in Continental
                    Europe, and France and the U.K. are the key allied countries in Europe. The
                    President has a limited amount of time and I did not think that for the purposes
                    of this exercise it was necessary to get into the Far East, Middle East or Latin
                    America. The real problem is how Europe looks at us for if we can maintain our
                    position of leadership with respect to Europe, we can do many things in the Near
                    East, and Far East particularly, which otherwise would be extremely
                    difficult.</p>
                <pb facs="0754" n="1544" xml:id="pg1544"/>
                <p>I gather you question the procedure in presenting this matter to the President
                    and I would be grateful for your guidance.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Douglas Mac Arthur
                    II</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d226-a1.xml" xpointer="d226-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d226-f1" n="1" xml:id="d226-f1-content">
                <p>Reference is presumably to those reports by Dillon and Aldrich discussed in the
                    Aug. 24, 1953, memorandum by Merchant, <ref target="#pg1468">p. 1468</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
